+## Dev Notes
+
+### Verified Twilio API contracts (ground-truthed against vendor docs 2026-08-08)
+
+**Send — Messages resource** ([Source: twilio.com/docs/messaging/api/message-resource]):
+- `POST https://api.twilio.com/2010-04-01/Accounts/{AccountSid}/Messages.json`
+- Content type: **`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`** (NOT JSON — confirmed `encodingType` in the spec). Mirror `stripe_client.form_encode` (`uri.percent_encode(k) <> "=" <> uri.percent_encode(pair.1)`, joined with `&`).
+- Body params this story uses: `From` (Alpha Sender ID string "RTenantry"), `To` (E.164), `Body`, `StatusCallback` (URL).
+- Auth: **HTTP Basic** with username=AccountSID, password=AuthToken → header `Authorization: Basic <base64(SID:AuthToken)>`. Build with `bit_array.from_string(sid <> ":" <> token) |> bit_array.base64_encode(False)` (precedent: `inbound_email/svix.gleam:66`).
+- Response: JSON with `sid` (`SM...`), `status` (`queued`/`sent`/`failed`/...), `error_code`, `error_message`.
+- The `From` field accepts an alphanumeric sender ID directly — confirmed by the spec ("The sender's Twilio phone number (in E.164 format), **alphanumeric sender ID**, ..."). So `TWILIO_ALPHA_SENDER="RTenantry"` goes in `From`. **This is why there is no `TWILIO_FROM`** (a from-number is impossible for IE SMS — Twilio IE numbers are voice-only; the Alpha Sender ID IS the sender).
+
+**Lookup — v2 API** ([Source: twilio.com/docs/lookup/v2-api]):
+- `GET https://lookups.twilio.com/v2/PhoneNumbers/{PhoneNumber}?Fields=line_type_intelligence`
+- `{PhoneNumber}` is E.164 in the PATH; the `+` MUST be percent-encoded → `%2B` (curl example uses `%2B14159929960`). Use `uri.percent_encode`.
+- Auth: same HTTP Basic (AccountSID:AuthToken). Same creds as send (AD-5: "Lookup reuses the same Account SID + Auth Token").
+- Response: `valid: bool`, `line_type_intelligence: null | { type: string, carrier_name: string, ... }`.
+- `line_type_intelligence.type` values: `mobile`, `landline`, `fixedVoIP`, `nonFixedVoIP`, `tollFree`, `premium`, `sharedCost`, `personal`, `unknown`, etc. This story maps to `{Mobile, Landline, VoIP, Unknown}` (AC3).
+- **Cost note:** `Fields=line_type_intelligence` is a paid data package. Double-check the param string in tests (a malformed `Fields` silently returns the free Basic Lookup with `line_type_intelligence: null`, which would mask a real bug).
+
+### Pattern fidelity — mirror these exactly
+
+- **`email_client.gleam`** (`server/src/notification/email_client.gleam`): the structural template. Pure `build_send_body` exposed for tests; `do_send` is the thin HTTP glue; `httpc.configure() |> httpc.timeout(10_000)` + `httpc.dispatch` (NEVER bare `httpc.send` — AGENTS.md mandates explicit timeouts and a grep lint bans the literal `httpc.send` in rationale comments); `Result(String, String)` return; `wisp.log_error` on failure.
+- **`sentry_client.{Enabled,Disabled}` + `init()`** (`server/src/sentry/sentry_client.gleam`): the Enabled/Disabled + init-from-env pattern. `sms_client.Twilio` mirrors this. Capture-with-context: `sentry_client.capture(sentry, BackgroundEvent(event_type:, message:, level: LevelError, tags: [...]))` — precedent `ai/ai_client.gleam:2069`, `reference_checks/trigger.gleam:984`, `notification/digest_handler.gleam:125`.
+- **`stripe_client.form_encode`** (`server/src/payment/stripe_client.gleam:610`): the form-body encoder. Reuse the same `uri.percent_encode` approach (do NOT re-implement percent-encoding).
+- **`ServerConfig` wiring** (`server/src/config.gleam` + `server/src/server.gleam:291`): add the field to the record; construct via `sms_client.init()` next to the other `*_client.init()` calls; pass it with label-shorthand `twilio:` in the `ServerConfig(...)` literal.
+
+### No-op-without-keys — the "shortlist email skipped" precedent (AC2/AC3)
+
+The established pattern (`notification/digest_handler.gleam`): when a dependency isn't configured, the code logs one line and returns a benign result rather than crashing. For sms_client that is `SendOutcome.Skipped`; for lookup that is `LineType.Unknown`. The caller never has to guard — the client owns the no-op. **Crucially, the no-op is NOT a Sentry event** (it's expected config state, not a failure); only real Twilio API failures capture (AC5). This is the difference between AC2 (no-op, silent) and AC5 (failure, captured).
+
+### Honesty note on `voip_or_burner` (AD-10)
+
+AD-10 lists the fraud signal as `line_type` / `voip_or_burner`. The Lookup **Line Type Intelligence** package returns `type` (mobile/landline/voip/...) and `carrier_name`. It does **NOT** return a "burner" flag — burner detection would need `sms_pumping_risk` (a different package) or a third-party vendor. Per AD-10's "honestly scoped to what's collectible today" principle, this story returns `line_type` + a `voip` boolean derived from it (`type` ∈ {fixedVoIP, nonFixedVoIP}). The `voip_or_burner` signal downstream (rc3-7) is honestly just `voip` from this source. Do NOT fabricate a burner flag. This matches the recurring finding: "never present fabricated detections as real".
+
+### Constraints (RT-specific — APPLY here)
+
+- **No em-dashes in user-facing copy** (RT global ban, CI-guarded). These modules have NO user-facing copy (log lines + JSON), but keep the em-dash ban in comments/docstrings too — the lint is repo-wide on text.
+- **Send FROM `TWILIO_ALPHA_SENDER` ("RTenantry") — NO from-number.** The one-way Alpha Sender ID reality (AD-5 corrected in PR #592, verified MERGED 2026-08-08).
+- **Explicit HTTP timeouts** — 10s for both send and lookup (`httpc.configure() |> httpc.timeout(10_000)` + `httpc.dispatch`). Never `httpc.send`/`send_bits`. Rationale comments must not contain the literal `httpc.send`.
+- **No `let assert`** in production code — use `case`. (`let assert` only in tests.)
+- **No JavaScript FFI.** Server Erlang FFI is fine; none needed here.
+- **Lookup fraud signals are display/confidence only — never auto-reject** (AD-10). This story doesn't make decisions, but the `LineType` type + the no-op-to-Unknown design enforce "never blocks the caller".
+- **Code builds + unit-tests without real Twilio creds.** The `Disabled` branch + the fact that `init()` returns `Disabled` when keys are absent is what makes this true. CI runs `make test` (unit only, no Docker) — it MUST be green with no Twilio env set.
